<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rimé movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rime movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rimé movement was a non-sectarian movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> that arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> in the 19th century. Led by Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamgon-kongtrul">Jamgön Kongtrul</a> and others, it collected and preserved teachings from all schools, especially lineages at risk of being lost, and encouraged respect between the Sakya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a>, Nyingma and other traditions. It enjoyed the patronage of the kings of Derge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rime-movement.jpg" alt="Rimé movement" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rime-letters.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Sam van Schaik, non-sectarianism was the norm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s golden age of the 14th and 15th centuries, when figures such as Longchenpa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a> studied with teachers of different schools. Sectarian wars in Central Tibet in the 16th and 17th centuries set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu schools</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a>, whose victory with the help of the Qoshot Mongol armies left the Gelug politically dominant and the minority lineages at risk of losing their traditions. Some Gelug figures kept a broader outlook, among them the Fifth Dalai Lama, who revered the Nyingma, and the yogi Shabkar Tsokdruk Rangdrol.</p><p>In the 19th century Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo (1820–1892), raised in the Sakya order, and Jamgön Kongtrül (1813–1899), of the Nyingma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a>, gathered and printed teachings of their schools, including many near-extinct ones, in compilations such as the Rinchen Terdzod and the Sheja Dzö.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name joins ris, side or bias, and med, absence, and means without distinction. Only in the 20th century did it become a name for the ideas of the 19th-century scholars, who did not form a conscious unified movement. The scholar Seyfort Ruegg called it universalistic and encyclopaedic rather than eclectic. As the teacher Ringu Tulku explains it, Rimé is not a new school and does not unite the schools by their similarities; it values their differences, and its followers usually keep one lineage as their main practice while respecting the others. Kongtrul warned that without a firm grounding in one's own tradition a student would become tangled, like a bad weaver.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Early Rimé writers often criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> tenets, which Georges Dreyfus reads as support for marginalised minority views. In the 20th century Phabongkha Rinpoche and his disciples, promoting the cult of Dorje Shugden and Gelug exclusivism, destroyed objects linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a> and forcibly converted Nyingma monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>. Rimé ideals have since been promoted by the 13th and 14th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>, the 15th and 16th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapas</a> and Sakya Trizin, and taking teachings from several lineages has become common.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rimé movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within Tibetan Buddhism, the <b>Rimé movement</b> encouraged friendlier attitudes between Bonpos and Buddhists.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century Derge patronised the non-sectarian <b>Rime movement</b> of teachers such as Jamgön Kongtrul.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-derge">Kingdom of Derge</a></li></ul></section>
